.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yume
is the YUME client. It opens an authenticated carrier to
.BR yumed (8)
and sends local application streams through that carrier. The mandatory 2.0
carrier remains HTTP/2 for the full connection and carries masked WebSocket
binary messages after a Chrome-shaped page/asset prime and RFC 8441 extended
CONNECT. Its mandatory inner channel combines ephemeral ML-KEM-1024, X25519,
and a random file PSK with salted HKDF, then uses one-use AES-256-GCM message
keys and independent directional epochs.
.PP
The client can expose a local SOCKS5 proxy, run one command through the
tunnel, create local or reverse forwards, attach to relay clients, or
use the directory and chat/file control features when the server permits
them.
.SH ROUTING MODEL
The common direct route is:
.PP
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  browser / curl                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  TLS / H2 / YUME frames        |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ==YUME==>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  direct egress                 |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees server IP                |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
The client network normally sees an HTTPS-looking connection to the YUME
server. The target normally sees the YUME server's egress IP. The server
process receives target metadata because it must open the outbound target
socket unless the application itself routes through another layer such as
Tor.
.SS Client through Tor to a hidden\-service yumed
With
.B \-\-tor
or
.B \-\-proxy socks5://host:port
the client routes the YUME carrier through a SOCKS5 proxy (typically the
local Tor daemon). The destination hostname is sent as ATYP_DOMAIN so
.B .onion
targets resolve on the proxy side; no DNS leaks. On the server,
.B yumed
binds 127.0.0.1 and Tor publishes a HiddenService that forwards onion
traffic to it.
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  browser / curl                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | SOCKS, --run, or forward
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  --tor / --proxy socks5://...  |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | SOCKS5 to <onion>:443
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  LOCAL TOR                     |
|  127.0.0.1:9050                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | encrypted cells
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR CIRCUIT                   |
|  hidden-service rendezvous     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | rendezvous on server
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  SERVER TOR                    |
|  hidden service publisher      |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | TLS 1.3 + YUME frames
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  binds 127.0.0.1 only          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | outbound socket
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees yumed egress IP          |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
.SS Server-side Tor egress
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  normal app                    |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  YUME carrier                  |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ==YUME==>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  receives target request       |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  SERVER TOR                    |
|  SOCKS / transparent route     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ...>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR ENTRY                     |
|  obfs4 bridge or guard         |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR MIDDLE                    |
|  relay nodes                   |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT                      |
|  target sees this IP           |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  website / API                 |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
This makes the target see a Tor exit instead of the YUME server, but the
.B yumed
process still receives the requested target unless the application itself
is only asking the server to connect to a Tor entry.
.SS Tor over YUME
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR BROWSER                   |
|  or app configured for Tor     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  LOCAL TOR                     |
|  builds circuit                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  relays Tor TCP                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ==YUME==>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  opens Tor entry only          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR ENTRY                     |
|  guard or obfs4 bridge         |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR NETWORK                   |
|  middle relays + exit          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees Tor exit                 |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
For target anonymity from the YUME server, prefer this route. The YUME
server usually sees only a Tor guard or bridge, not the final website.
.SS YUME over Tor
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  normal app                    |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  carrier source                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  CLIENT TOR                    |
|  carries YUME carrier          |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | ...>
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT                      |
|  client-side edge              |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  sees Tor exit                 |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees server IP                |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
This hides the client's real IP from the YUME server, but it is slower
and the Tor exit can see that a connection is being made to the YUME
server.
.SS Tor -> YUME -> Tor
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  HUMAN APP                     |
|  normal app                    |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME CLIENT                   |
|  carrier source                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | YUME carrier through client Tor
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  CLIENT TOR                    |
|  obfs4 optional                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT A                    |
|  client-side edge              |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | YUME carrier reaches server
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUMED SERVER                  |
|  sees Tor exit A               |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        | target stream through server Tor
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  SERVER TOR                    |
|  obfs4 optional                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TOR EXIT B                    |
|  target-side edge              |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  TARGET SITE                   |
|  sees exit B                   |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
This can reduce linkability for passive observers because neither edge
sees both the real client and the final target. It is also much slower
and does not hide the requested target from
.B yumed
unless the application itself uses Tor over YUME.
.SS Common routes summary
.nf
Fast path:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> target

Server-side Tor egress:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> server Tor ...> target

Tor over YUME:
  Tor Browser -> local Tor -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> Tor entry ...> target

YUME over Tor:
  app -> yume -> client Tor ...> yumed -> target

Tor -> YUME -> Tor:
  app -> yume -> client Tor ...> yumed -> server Tor ...> target

VPN/proxy after YUME:
  app -> yume ==YUME==> yumed -> VPN/proxy -> target
.fi
.SH TRANSPORT LAYERS
.SS Carrier frame shape
.nf
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  OUTER TLS 1.3 CARRIER                                               |
|                                                                      |
|  YUME frame header                                                   |
|    type, flags, stream id                                            |
|                                                                      |
|  INNER PAYLOAD                                                       |
|    encrypted with BaseFWX / AES-256-GCM                              |
|    authenticated with frame context                                  |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
Inner encryption protects YUME frame payloads between the client and
server. It does not replace application HTTPS and does not hide target
metadata from a server that is asked to open the target directly.
.SS HTTP/2 obfs sequence
.nf
+--------------------------------+
|  TLS HANDSHAKE                 |
|  real TLS 1.3                  |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  HTTP/2 PREFACE                |
|  PRI * HTTP/2.0                |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  PROJECT H2 SETTINGS           |
|  SETTINGS + WINDOW_UPDATE      |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  OBFUSCATED REQUEST            |
|  POST /token/nonce HEADERS     |
+--------------------------------+
        |
        v
+--------------------------------+
|  YUME FRAMES                   |
|  carrier data begins           |
+--------------------------------+
.fi
.PP
The obfs path uses a standards-conformant HTTP/2 opening before the YUME
carrier begins. Its settings and headers are project templates, not
byte-identical output from a named browser, and the later carrier is not a
full-session HTTP/2 tunnel.
.SS Stream multiplexing
.nf
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  ONE AUTHENTICATED CARRIER                                           |
|                                                                      |
|  stream 1  OPEN ifconfig.me:80  -> DATA -> DATA <- CLOSE             |
|  stream 2  OPEN 1.1.1.1:443   -> app TLS bytes <-> target bytes      |
|  stream 3  OPEN api.site:443  -> another independent connection      |
+----------------------------------------------------------------------+
.fi
.SH MODES
.TP
.BI "--socks " [addr:]port
Start a local SOCKS5 proxy. The optional
.I addr
must be an IP literal, for example
.BR 127.0.0.1:1080 ,
.BR 0.0.0.0:1080 ,
or
.BR [::1]:1080 .
Without an address YUME keeps the historical wildcard bind. Use
.B socks5h://
in clients that support it so DNS is resolved through YUME.
.TP
.BI "-L " [bind:]lport:host:port
Open an SSH-style local forward. Local connections to
.I lport
are relayed through YUME to
.IR host : port .
The optional
.I bind
must be an IP literal.
.TP
.BI "-R " [bind:]rport:host:port
Open a reverse forward. The server listens on
.I rport
and relays accepted connections back to the client's
.IR host : port .
The optional
.I bind
must be an IP literal on the server side.
.TP
.BI "--run " command
Run a local command with its TCP/UDP sockets routed through YUME.
.TP
.B --directory
List visible relay endpoints from the authenticated server.
.TP
.BI "--chat " id-or-name
Start a chat with a relay endpoint when allowed by policy.
.TP
.BI "--send-file " id-or-name " " path
Send a file through the relay channel when allowed by policy.
.TP
.BI "--send-bytes " id-or-name " " path
Send raw bytes through the relay channel when allowed by policy.
.TP
.BI "--admin-attach " id-or-name
Open a trusted admin channel to a relay endpoint when both sides permit it.
.TP
.BI "--control " [id]
Attach to a controlled client.
.TP
.B --attach-local
Attach to a local running YUME instance.
.SH CONNECTION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--server " host
Server hostname or IP address.
.TP
.BI "--port " port
Server port. If omitted, the client uses the configured/default server
port.
.TP
.BI "-i, --auth " path
Composite Ed25519 + ML-DSA-87 identity used for server authentication.
.TP
.BI "--admin-auth " path
Distinct composite second identity for an admin session. It must be enrolled in
the server's admin store; presenting this key is the admin claim.
.TP
.BI "--config " path
Read client configuration from a JSON file.
.TP
.BI "--threads " n
I/O thread count. Use
.B 0
for automatic selection.
.TP
.BI "--rekey-window " n
Concurrent directional epoch offers this client accepts, and, capped by the
server's advertised depth, the number it keeps prepared for sending. Range
.BR 1 - 64 ,
default
.BR 8 .
Each prepared epoch adds one negotiated epoch byte budget per rekey round trip
on high-latency links. Under the default Extreme mode that budget is 256 KiB,
with independent limits of 512 frames and 500 ms sender-active time. The JSON
configuration key
.B security_mode
selects Extreme, Normal, Soft, or a complete bounded Ultimate policy; see
.BR docs/SECURITY_MODES.md .
.TP
.B --udp
Enable UDP forwarding.
.TP
.B --tcp
Force TCP-only behavior.
.TP
.B --allow-local-ip
Allow private, loopback, and link-local destinations where policy permits.
.TP
.B --run-ipv4
Prefer IPv4 behavior for
.BR --run .
.TP
.B --root
Keep root privileges instead of dropping them where supported.
.SH SECURITY OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--obfs-secret-file " path
Load the mandatory admission secret. The file must contain exactly 64
lowercase hexadecimal characters encoding 32 random bytes, with no newline
and no group/world permissions.
.TP
.BI "--inner-psk-file " path
Load the separate mandatory inner PSK in the same strict file format. It is
mixed by salted HKDF; Argon2 is not used. Ratchet modes never disable the
mandatory hybrid exchange, HKDF, or per-message AES-256-GCM keys.
.TP
.B "--require-operator-identity"
Require proof that the endpoint is authorized by the selected operator CA.
This authenticates the operator; it does not prove no logging or anonymity.
.TP
.BI "--operator-ca-cert " path
Operator CA certificate used to verify endpoint authorization.
.TP
.BI "--tls-ca " path
TLS CA certificate.
.TP
.BI "--tls-pin " sha256
Pin the server TLS certificate fingerprint.
.SH TLS STEALTH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--profile " name
Select the pinned Chrome 151 Linux profile. In 2.0-dev6,
.B chrome
is the only accepted value.
.TP
.BI "--transport-profile " id
Select the authenticated dev6 transport identity. The only accepted value is
.BR chrome151-node24-v1 .
.TP
.BI "--tls-backend " name
Select
.B chrome151
for the packaged Linux helper or
.B openssl-diagnostic
for the explicit non-Chrome-parity diagnostic path. There is no silent
fallback.
.TP
.BI "--tls-helper " path
Use an explicit absolute helper path. The launcher requires a regular
executable owned by root or the current user and rejects group/world-writable
files.
.TP
.B "--hide-in-the-crowd chrome"
Explicit spelling for the same pinned Chrome 151 HTTP User-Agent profile.
Other HTTP profiles are rejected for 2.0-dev6.
.TP
.B --tls-fingerprint-log
Log TLS fingerprint metrics.
.TP
.BI "--tls-fingerprint-log-path " path
Path for TLS fingerprint log output.
.TP
.B --tls-fingerprint-verify
Verify fingerprints against a test endpoint.
.TP
.BI "--tls-fingerprint-test-endpoint " host
Host used for fingerprint verification tests.
.SH RELAY OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--name " slug
Display name for this relay endpoint.
.TP
.BI "--client-id " 32hex
Stable client endpoint ID.
.TP
.BI "--relay-mode " mode
Relay trust mode:
.B untrusted
or
.BR trusted .
.TP
.B "--allow-inbound-admin, --deny-inbound-admin"
Allow or deny inbound admin attach requests.
.TP
.B "--allow-outbound-admin, --deny-outbound-admin"
Allow or deny outbound admin attach requests.
.TP
.B "--allow-chat, --deny-chat"
Allow or deny chat relay features.
.TP
.B "--allow-file, --deny-file"
Allow or deny file relay features.
.TP
.B "--allow-bytes, --deny-bytes"
Allow or deny byte relay features.
.TP
.BI "--relay-key-file " path
Relay key file.
.SH RUNTIME OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--instance " name
Runtime instance name.
.TP
.BI "--history-dir " path
Chat history directory.
.TP
.B --no-history
Disable chat history.
.TP
.B --non-interactive
Disable live status redraw.
.TP
.B --live-status
Enable live status redraw.
.TP
.B --timing
Emit precise timing diagnostics in a Debug or RelWithDebInfo developer build.
Release and MinSizeRel binaries compile these hooks out and warn that the
option is unavailable.
.TP
.B --boring
Use minimal output.
.TP
.B --
Service-safe launch separator.
.SH INTERACTIVE CONSOLE
When a session stays attached, the console accepts commands such as:
.PP
.nf
    help, status, directory, invites, chat, send, send-file, send-bytes,
    accept, reject, history, history-delete, admin attach, admin status,
    admin sessions, admin stop, whoami, quit
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start a local SOCKS5 proxy:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --socks 1080
.fi
.TP
Use curl with remote DNS through the proxy:
.nf
curl -x socks5h://127.0.0.1:1080 https://ifconfig.me
.fi
.TP
Run one command through YUME:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --run "curl https://1.1.1.1"
.fi
.TP
Create a local forward:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 -L 2222:127.0.0.1:22
.fi
.TP
Require operator identity proof:
.nf
yume --server origin.example.net -i ~/.yume/id_ed25519 --socks 1080 \
     --require-operator-identity --operator-ca-cert /etc/yume/operator-ca.pem
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Inner encryption protects YUME frame payloads between the client and
server. It is not browser-to-target end-to-end encryption; HTTPS in the
application still provides that layer.
.PP
The YUME server needs enough target metadata to open outbound sockets.
If the server must not learn final targets, run Tor locally and route Tor
through YUME.
.PP
Use deployment-specific PQ key files.
.PP
Operator identity proof verifies a CA-authorized endpoint and the associated
mode asks the server to minimize identifying logs. It cannot prove that the
operator follows that policy, and does not remove hosting logs, payment
records, DNS records, browser fingerprints, logged-in accounts, or
time-correlation risks.
